component setliteraldefault
export Executable

(* A SET LITERAL WHOSE ELEMENTS ARE ALL LITERALS OF ONE KIND takes that kind as
   its element type. `3 IN {0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5}` is the corpus witness
   (`SpecData/examples/basic/Expr.Set.fss`) and it is the whole reason this rule
   exists.

   IT IS NOT INFERENCE. It reads the SHAPE of what was written, which is what
   lets it run where it has to run: monomorphization stamps `Set[\T\]` BEFORE
   `Checker::new`, so a type discoverable only by TYPING the elements cannot be
   stamped at all. A literal needs no typing to be recognised.

   An integer literal defaults to ZZ32, which is the fallback the checker
   already applies to a bare integer -- so the `3` on the left of `IN` and the
   element type agree instead of disagreeing.

   A STRING SET IS NOT EXERCISED HERE AND THAT IS NOT AN OVERSIGHT. `opr IN`
   over a String set is broken independently of this rule -- an EXPLICITLY
   typed `{[\String\] "a", "b"}` fails the same way, with `condition must be
   Boolean, found String` -- so a String row would be asserting someone else's
   bug. The default itself produces `String` correctly. *)
run(): () = do
  println(3 IN {0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5})
  println(9 IN {0, 1, 2})
  println(true IN {false})
end

end
